## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design of an AWS AI pipeline handling privacy, scaling, and cost. Combines managed AI services (Comprehend, SageMaker) with event-driven wiring (S3, Lambda, DynamoDB).

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture across S3, Lambda, and SageMaker
- AI services integration: Comprehend for NER/sentiment, SageMaker for topic modeling
- Data privacy: PII redaction, encryption at rest/in transit, access controls
- Cost awareness: batching, cold starts, endpoint usage, and streaming vs. per-file processing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: demonstrate sequence of calls to AWS services
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function analyze(text){
  const entities = await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const sentiment = await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName:'topic-model', Body: JSON.stringify({text}), ContentType:'application/json'}).promise();
  return {entities, sentiment, topics: JSON.parse(res.Body.toString())};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures and retries across Lambda, SageMaker, and DynamoDB?
- What security controls and monitoring would you add for regulatory compliance (e.g., encryption, audit logs, IAM least privilege)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a secure, cost-conscious AWS-native pipeline that handles PII responsibly using services like S3, Lambda, Comprehend, and DynamoDB, with proper error handling and auditing.

## Key Concepts
- PII detection: Amazon Comprehend PII entities
- Data flow: S3 -> Lambda -> S3 (redacted) -> DynamoDB
- Privacy controls: encryption at rest (SSE/KMS), data minimization
- Reliability: dead-letter queues (DLQ), retries, idempotent processing, logging

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend({region: 'us-east-1'});
async function redact(text) {
  const res = await comprehend.detectPiiEntities({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'en' }).promise();
  let redacted = text;
  // naive approach: replace ranges from end to start to avoid offset shifts
  const ranges = res.Entities.map(e => ({ s: e.BeginOffset, e: e.EndOffset }));
  ranges.sort((a,b) => b.s - a.s);
  for (const r of ranges) {
    redacted = redacted.substring(0, r.s) + '[REDACTED]' + redacted.substring(r.e);
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate redaction accuracy and handle false positives/negatives? 
- How would you adapt this for higher throughput or multilingual transcripts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end understanding of an AWS-based AI data flow, including extraction, NLP, storage, and indexing, plus privacy and cost controls.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven design with S3, Lambda, Textract, Comprehend, DynamoDB
- IAM least privilege and KMS encryption
- Observability: DLQ, retries, CloudWatch
- Data governance: PII detection, versioning guidance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo Lambda handler sketch
exports.handler = async (evt) => {
  const s3Key = evt.Records[0].s3.object.key;
  const text = await textract.extractText(s3Key);
  const topics = await comprehend.classify(text);
  const pii = comprehend.detectPII(text);
  await s3.writeJSON(text, s3Key.replace('input','output'));
  await dynamo.upsert({ designId, s3Key, transcriptKey: '...', topics, piiFlag: pii > 0, timestamp: Date.now()});
}
```

